`authentication_contract` may be null, but when present its `scheme` must name the actual
authentication mechanism concisely -- `bearer`, `basic`, `apiKey`, or a comparably short token a
client library would recognize -- never a descriptive sentence about it. `request_schema` and
`schema_definition` may be
null. `api_contracts`, `data_entities`, `risks`, `event_contracts`, and
`environment_variables` may be empty arrays. Every item in `query_parameters`,
`path_parameters`, and `headers` must be a JSON object, never a plain string; use an empty
array when there are none. All listed repository IDs and workstreams must match the submitted
repositories exactly; assign every submitted repository exactly one workstream. Every technical
requirement ID must be assigned to at least one required workstream. A requirement ID is only
ever a `requirement_id` value taken from the submitted technical PRD's requirement lists. The
names of the PRD's own sections, such as `security_requirements` or `functional_requirements`,
are containers and are never requirement IDs; never place one in `requirement_ids`,
`scoped_requirements`, `shared_requirements`, or `out_of_scope_requirements`. Every new workstream must
include `scoped_requirements`, `out_of_scope_requirements`, and `shared_requirements`; each scoped
reference must include deterministic `requirement_id:ac-N` acceptance IDs. A workstream's
`requirement_ids` must contain exactly the `requirement_id` values of its own
`scoped_requirements`, with no extra and none missing: the two fields describe the same set, so
whenever you add or remove a scoped reference you must make the same change to `requirement_ids`. A requirement may be
present in multiple repositories when it is a shared integration concern; every reference must
state that repository's explicit responsibility. A backend and frontend may each implement their
own side of a shared API contract. Do not scope a requirement that is listed as out of scope.
The integration contract is approved and immutable before any repository starts work, so
repositories that only consume it are implemented in parallel. Use
`dependency_workstream_ids` only when a repository must compile or link against another
repository's published build artifact, such as a shared library. Never make a repository
wait merely because it calls another repository's API, and never use it to express a
preferred merge or deployment sequence: put that in `recommended_merge_order` instead.
Scheduling is recomputed deterministically from these dependencies, so an unjustified
dependency will be discarded.
{%- if plan_task_dependencies | default(false) %}
A workstream's `task_ids` and `responsibilities` are one list of tasks written as two arrays:
the first task_id names the task the first responsibility describes, the second names the
second, and so on. Write them in the same order and give them the same length. Split a
workstream into more than one task only where the work genuinely separates into pieces that
could each be implemented and reviewed on their own.
Use `task_dependencies` to say that one of that workstream's tasks cannot be started until
another of its tasks is finished. Every `task_id` and every `depends_on` entry must be a task
declared in that same workstream's `task_ids`: this is ordering inside one repository, and
ordering between repositories is the `dependency_workstream_ids` question above. Declare a
dependency only where the later task would have nothing to work against until the earlier one
exists -- the module it imports, the schema it reads, the table its query needs. Never make a
task wait merely because it is written later in the list, because it touches the same files,
or because it reads as the next step of the same story: those tasks are independent, and
saying otherwise puts them in a queue for no reason. A workstream whose every task waits for
the one before it has described a sequence rather than a dependency, and says exactly as much
as declaring none. Declaring none is the ordinary case: use the empty array, or omit
`task_dependencies` entirely, when the tasks can be done in any order. An edge naming a task
the workstream does not declare, a task waiting on itself, or a set of tasks waiting on each
other is discarded.
{%- endif %}
Every name in `contract_sections_implemented` and `contract_sections_consumed` must be defined
in the `integration_contract` of this same response: an `operation_id`, a `shared_schemas` name,
an `event_contracts` event_name, an `error_contracts` code, or an `environment_variables` name.
These strings are given to the engineer as its instructions and to the reviewer as its scope, so
a name that is not in the contract asks somebody to build a section that does not exist. Use the
empty array when a workstream implements or consumes nothing.
Each workstream's `acceptance_criteria` are checked by a reviewer reading that repository's diff
and the output of its own configured commands. Write only criteria that can be met that way. Never
write a criterion asking for manual verification, visual inspection, someone observing the running
application, or a measurement against a deployment: no implementation attempt can satisfy one, so
the workstream is rejected for it however complete the work is, until its budget runs out. State
the behaviour the code must have instead.

Every scoped requirement needs an `implementation_expectations` entry.
{% if has_reconnaissance %}
Each repository has been checked out and read, and the reconnaissance below reports what it
actually contains. Ground the plan in it. `expected_source_areas` must name directories that
appear in that repository's `source_areas`; when a requirement adds something of a kind the
repository already has a convention for, follow that convention and name its `wiring_path` as a
source area too, so the new code is registered rather than merely written. Do not name a path
that is absent from the reconnaissance, and do not infer a language, framework, package manager,
or layout from a repository's role when the evidence says otherwise.

Read `contradicted_premises` before assigning anything. Each one is a requirement premise the
checkout does not support, and a workstream scoped to it cannot be completed by any attempt: the
implementation would be rejected for failing a criterion nothing in the repository can satisfy.
For each contradicted premise, either scope the requirement to what the repository can actually
support and say so in the workstream's responsibility, or leave the requirement out of scope for
that repository. Never assign a requirement whose premise the evidence contradicts and hope the
engineer resolves it.

Each reconnaissance also carries `runnable_validation_commands`: every command this platform will
run against that checkout, and all of them. A `test_requirements` entry that asks for a command to
be RUN may name only commands on that list. The engineer cannot add a command to it and neither
can the reviewer, so a requirement naming anything else is one no attempt can ever satisfy -- and
the review will hold the workstream to it, round after round, while the code it is rejecting is
finished. AB-Feature-225 required "the applicable Playwright commands" of a checkout that has a
Playwright suite the platform does not run; it was rejected three times, in three different
wordings, with nine of its ten requirements passing.

That a repository *contains* a test framework is not evidence the platform runs it. When the
coverage a requirement wants can only be executed by a command absent from that list, ask for the
coverage to be WRITTEN and say plainly that its execution is not part of this workstream's gate.
An empty `runnable_validation_commands` means the set was not established for that repository, not
that nothing runs: fall back to describing the coverage rather than naming any command.
{% else %}
Repository links are supplied before their contents are checked out, so do not infer a language,
framework, package manager, or directory layout from a repository role. Use the generic
`production` category (plus `test` when tests are required) and leave `expected_source_areas`
empty unless the submitted PRD explicitly names an exact repository-relative path.
{% endif %}
Empty means the empty array `[]`. No array anywhere in
this response may contain an empty string: drop the entry instead of emitting `""`, because every
string in these arrays must name something real. Tests alone cannot satisfy a production requirement. Add
new test infrastructure only when the plan explicitly justifies its dependency and configuration
changes. Preserve stable operation IDs, schema names,
paths, error codes, and existing authentication behavior. Do not include secrets.
